  "prediction": "The Atomic Energy Act (AEA) of 1954 requires a peaceful nuclear cooperation agreement between the United States and other countries for significant nuclear transfers. The AEA covers the export of nuclear materials, technology, and services. Section 123 of the AEA outlines the process for negotiating and implementing these agreements, which includes congressional approval.\n\nThe report details the requirements for Section 123 agreements, including their nonproliferation criteria, the role of the Department of State, and the congressional approval process. The AEA sets forth specific nonproliferation criteria that must be met for an agreement to be approved, such as the recipient country's commitment to nonproliferation and peaceful uses of nuclear technology.\n\nThe report also discusses the different types of nuclear cooperation agreements, including those that require congressional approval and those that do not. The report provides examples of nuclear cooperation agreements that have been approved outside of the AEA process, such as the U.S.-Australia agreement and the U.S.-India agreement.\n\nThe report concludes by noting that the AEA provides a framework for peaceful nuclear cooperation while ensuring that nonproliferation objectives are met. The report emphasizes the importance of the AEA in promoting international cooperation on nuclear energy and nonproliferation efforts.",
  "scored_prediction": "The Atomic Energy Act (AEA) of 1954 requires a peaceful nuclear cooperation agreement between the United States and other countries for significant nuclear transfers. The AEA covers the export of nuclear materials, technology, and services. Section 123 of the AEA outlines the process for negotiating and implementing these agreements, which includes congressional approval.\n\nThe report details the requirements for Section 123 agreements, including their nonproliferation criteria, the role of the Department of State, and the congressional approval process. The AEA sets forth specific nonproliferation criteria that must be met for an agreement to be approved, such as the recipient country's commitment to nonproliferation and peaceful uses of nuclear technology.\n\nThe report also discusses the different types of nuclear cooperation agreements, including those that require congressional approval and those that do not. The report provides examples of nuclear cooperation agreements that have been approved outside of the AEA process, such as the U.S.-Australia agreement and the U.S.-India agreement.\n\nThe report concludes by noting that the AEA provides a framework for peaceful nuclear cooperation while ensuring that nonproliferation objectives are met. The report emphasizes the importance of the AEA in promoting international cooperation on nuclear energy and nonproliferation efforts.",
  "score": 29.069766966265554,
  "answers": [
    "In order for the United States to engage in significant civilian nuclear cooperation with other states, it must conclude a framework agreement that meets specific requirements under Section 123 of the Atomic Energy Act (AEA). Significant nuclear cooperation includes the export of reactors, critical parts of reactors, and reactor fuel. The AEA also provides for export control licensing procedures and criteria for terminating cooperation. Congressional review is required for Section 123 agreements; the AEA establishes special parliamentary procedures by which Congress may act on a proposed agreement."
  ],
